																		Back and wanting to overhaul the experiment log.
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/spaztique" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304338); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=304338&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608887500" alt="Spaztique" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=304338)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/spaztique" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304338); return false;" >Spaztique</a></span> <span class="odate time_1472443574 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Aug 2016 04:06</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2578023">
									

<p>Hey guys. It's been a loooong while, and I return to my most successful SCP with a goal to revise the experiment log.</p>
<p>When I originally envisioned this page, it was going to be a more light-hearted side of SCP-624, but what really makes this SCP successful, as you can see with my later edits of the original test subject and the audiobook lady, the real fun (and horror) of this SCP comes from the results it gets from people who don't listen to music.</p>
<p>So, I'm thinking of deleting or altering the more comical logs and replacing them with specifically horror-only or weird-only entries. Let me know what you guys think. I'll begin revisions in a few days. Anyone else is welcome to jump in on this, too: after all, none of the SCPs I ever made ever got off the ground without everyone else's help.</p>

							</div>

								
					
